00:06:09.590 --> 00:06:12.530
ANDREAS SEKINE: So before
GCP we had a Hadoop cluster

00:06:12.530 --> 00:06:15.200
that we were managing
ourselves in AWS,

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:18.612
and that worked very well for
a year and a half, two years.

00:06:18.612 --> 00:06:20.570
We started hitting some
real growing pains when

00:06:20.570 --> 00:06:23.120
it came to scale and a lot
of the static versioning

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:26.237
of every time we
needed more capacity

00:06:26.237 --> 00:06:28.070
we'd have to manually
purge the new cluster,

00:06:28.070 --> 00:06:31.260
bring it up, do an
HDFS re-balance.

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:35.550
We had a couple of days then
it could start being utilized.

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:38.490
We had very spiky
utilization of our resources.

00:06:38.490 --> 00:06:41.990
So all of the ad hoc
analysis or ad hoc queries

00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:45.549
that were being run were
being run on Hive and Impala.

00:06:45.549 --> 00:06:47.090
Those would be
conflicting because it

00:06:47.090 --> 00:06:50.000
was sharing resources with
Yarn and the Spark jobs

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:51.020
were running.

00:06:51.020 --> 00:06:54.380
So just this really
inflexible infrastructure

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:56.450
made it so that we
wanted something

00:06:56.450 --> 00:06:59.300
that could grow more dynamically
as our data would grow.

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:02.000
And GCP had some
very nice offerings

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:03.980
in terms of having
Dataproc clusters that

00:07:03.980 --> 00:07:07.060
can be ephemeral and be spun
up in 90 seconds, run your job

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:08.740
and spin back down.

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:11.660
BigQuery, where we can just kind
of pass the buck on to Google

00:07:11.660 --> 00:07:13.368
and let them deal with
all the operations

00:07:13.368 --> 00:07:16.760
overhead, because being on call
at this time was a huge pain.

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:18.830
It would be, basically
most of your week on call

00:07:18.830 --> 00:07:21.090
would be just putting out fires.

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:22.839
So it was a huge win for us.

00:07:22.839 --> 00:07:23.630
FELIPE HOFFA: Cool.

00:07:23.630 --> 00:07:25.100
How about Tapjoy?

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:26.710
What's your, like, the--

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:28.130
ROBIN LI: Yeah.

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:33.080
I think a lot of people bring
up a very good point, which

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:35.060
is, you know, data
[INAUDIBLE] is not only

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:36.360
about moving data around.

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:39.410
It's also about what tools to
choose and what to what tools

00:07:39.410 --> 00:07:40.400
to utilize, right?

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:46.430
And I think up to that
point we previously

00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:51.890
had a very large on-premises
data center we built ourselves.

00:07:51.890 --> 00:07:54.470
We basically bought up
a couple million dollars

00:07:54.470 --> 00:07:57.465
worth of hardware,
strung them together,

00:07:57.465 --> 00:08:02.660
outsourced a company to do all
our offline smart [INAUDIBLE]

00:08:02.660 --> 00:08:03.590
works.

00:08:03.590 --> 00:08:06.140
And threw it into a
[INAUDIBLE] data center

00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:07.880
right next to
[INAUDIBLE] internally

00:08:07.880 --> 00:08:10.370
through with direct
connect fiber link.

00:08:10.370 --> 00:08:12.430
All of that work is
pretty cool, but it

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:14.460
has a lot of operational
overheads, right?

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:19.010
And we have our 300- to
400-machine Hadoop production

00:08:19.010 --> 00:08:20.000
cluster.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:23.690
Typically sit on
bare-metal hardware.

00:08:23.690 --> 00:08:26.300
If any of those goes offline we
have to deal with them, right?

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:30.710
So and then after we
started looking at BigQuery,

00:08:30.710 --> 00:08:33.265
after we move our entire
PB solution in the BigQuery

00:08:33.265 --> 00:08:36.200
we realized that
as a company size

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:40.850
of Tapjoy, which is around
200 to 300 people it's

00:08:40.850 --> 00:08:43.750
pretty impressive to do
on-premises data center

00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:44.550
is one thing.

00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:46.370
But on the other
thing is we need

00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:48.770
to think about operational
overhead as well

00:08:48.770 --> 00:08:53.130
as the true running cost
for your infrastructure.

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:55.340
So that's why we're
starting to looking

00:08:55.340 --> 00:08:59.240
at moving our entire
infrastructure to cloud based

00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:02.350
and we did a lot of
benchmarking and comparison.

00:09:02.350 --> 00:09:05.440
My Google account reps
helped me a lot with that.

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:08.960
And at the end the day
it works really well.

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:13.310
So we merged our entire
Hadoop infrastructure

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:16.556
from on-premises data
center into Google Cloud.

00:09:16.556 --> 00:09:21.290
But a little bit different
from this gentleman's usage.

00:09:21.290 --> 00:09:22.460
We didn't use Dataproc.

00:09:22.460 --> 00:09:27.850
We basically built our own
elastic Hadoop Spark cluster

00:09:27.850 --> 00:09:32.420
into one on Google Cloud,
utilizing the cloud power

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:35.480
for a very spiky workload.

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:37.233
That's before and after.

00:09:37.233 --> 00:09:38.220
ANDREI SAVU: Cool.

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:41.750
LUIS BITENCOURT: So we
were on Hive before.

00:09:41.750 --> 00:09:44.464
I've always been a fan of not
building my own infra when

00:09:44.464 --> 00:09:45.130
I don't need to.

00:09:45.130 --> 00:09:46.580
Like, data infrastructure
is not going

00:09:46.580 --> 00:09:47.690
to be the differentiator
for Reddit,

00:09:47.690 --> 00:09:49.773
it's not going to be what
makes it succeed or not.

00:09:49.773 --> 00:09:51.710
So we were using Qubole
which is kind of cool,

00:09:51.710 --> 00:09:54.980
but the difference between
Qubole and BigQuery

00:09:54.980 --> 00:09:57.890
is essentially like,
picture driving a manual car

00:09:57.890 --> 00:10:01.400
in the '60s when you had to do
a lot of work and maintenance

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:04.340
and checking the engine oil
and doing all that stuff,

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:06.402
versus an automatic car
in this day and age where

00:10:06.402 --> 00:10:08.860
you don't have take it in for
a checkup until, like, 50,000

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:10.280
miles.

00:10:10.280 --> 00:10:15.860
So its even less overhead on
the engineering side, and it

00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:18.820
helped with some of the
speed concerns that we had.

00:10:18.820 --> 00:10:21.629
That said, you have to actually
structure your data cognizantly

00:10:21.629 --> 00:10:24.170
of some of-- especially when
you have the amount of data that

00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:25.010
Reddit has--

00:10:25.010 --> 00:10:26.180
of some of the limitations.

00:10:26.180 --> 00:10:28.010
Because even
BigQuery has limits.

00:10:28.010 --> 00:10:28.891
Everyone has limits.

00:10:28.891 --> 00:10:29.390
Uh, yeah.

00:10:29.390 --> 00:10:30.624
Sorry.

00:10:30.624 --> 00:10:33.040
So there is some thinking that
you have to do around that.

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:35.300
And there are some
differences with the abilities

00:10:35.300 --> 00:10:37.050
that you have in Hive
versus what you have

00:10:37.050 --> 00:10:38.240
in BigQuery to acknowledge.

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:42.410
But it's certainly a lot
less maintenance overhead

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:45.820
than we had even in Qubole,
which theoretically is much

00:10:45.820 --> 00:10:47.390
easier than running your own--

00:10:47.390 --> 00:10:50.740
which is much easier than
running your own Hive clusters.

00:10:50.740 --> 00:10:53.740
But was still-- basically
the speed of iteration

00:10:53.740 --> 00:10:55.614
was the main reason
to try it out.

00:10:55.614 --> 00:10:58.030
Being able to write a query,
run it and get it back really

00:10:58.030 --> 00:11:01.000
quickly versus waiting two
minutes for Qubole hive

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:04.270
clusters to spin up, or
having Presto clusters

00:11:04.270 --> 00:11:06.700
that were big enough and
that your SQL query was

00:11:06.700 --> 00:11:09.620
optimized enough to actually
be able to run against Presto.

00:11:09.620 --> 00:11:12.340
BitQuery just got some of
those concerns away from us.

00:11:12.340 --> 00:11:14.830
And there were more
tools that out of the box

00:11:14.830 --> 00:11:16.270
were built to support it.

00:11:16.270 --> 00:11:20.230
So things like mode analytics,
Looker, Periscope data,

00:11:20.230 --> 00:11:22.314
what you can just
point to BigQuery

00:11:22.314 --> 00:11:23.980
in the cloud versus
having to figure out

00:11:23.980 --> 00:11:26.770
how you get your
clusters that Qubole spin

00:11:26.770 --> 00:11:29.520
up somewhere to feed up to some
of these data analytics tools,

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:30.546
so.

00:11:30.546 --> 00:11:32.960
ANDREI SAVU: Awesome.

