00:17:10.730 --> 00:17:14.720
such as the stack drivers,
which produced a log [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:14.720 --> 00:17:19.460
as well as all of the
APIs that Google supports.

00:17:19.460 --> 00:17:21.920
One other good, very
interesting example,

00:17:21.920 --> 00:17:27.260
which is good BigQuery is
prising at per-query level.

00:17:27.260 --> 00:17:29.270
How much data you
moved per query.

00:17:29.270 --> 00:17:34.160
So its eliminated a lot of
DVME work and infrastructure

00:17:34.160 --> 00:17:34.900
operational work.

00:17:34.900 --> 00:17:38.330
But a new type of work which
is introduced by BigQuery,

00:17:38.330 --> 00:17:39.010
slightly--

00:17:39.010 --> 00:17:42.050
I'm sorry-- but it is how do
you curb your costs, right?

00:17:42.050 --> 00:17:44.550
So how do you keep your
costs at a minimal level,

00:17:44.550 --> 00:17:47.570
making sure your users are
running meaningful queries

00:17:47.570 --> 00:17:48.980
to get their results?

00:17:48.980 --> 00:17:52.450
So we are calling
all the Google APIs

00:17:52.450 --> 00:17:55.790
to get all the queries, usage,
per-user, per-query level,

00:17:55.790 --> 00:17:56.990
normalizes them.

00:17:56.990 --> 00:17:59.210
You know, stripe
out the dates part,

00:17:59.210 --> 00:18:02.450
the common [INAUDIBLE] parts
and putting them into a BigQuery

00:18:02.450 --> 00:18:06.790
table for analyzing BigQuery
costs, which actually--

00:18:06.790 --> 00:18:07.290
yeah.

00:18:07.290 --> 00:18:10.599
It's kind of like a feedback
