leap to a streaming world

00:24:15.930 --> 00:24:17.320
is pretty daunting.

00:24:17.320 --> 00:24:19.680
And it takes a lot
of upfront thought

00:24:19.680 --> 00:24:21.910
about how to make that switch.

00:24:21.910 --> 00:24:26.310
And with the rise of Apache Beam
and Spark structured streaming,

00:24:26.310 --> 00:24:30.810
that's becoming a lot easier,
and I'm excited to see that.

00:24:30.810 --> 00:24:32.010
ANDREI SAVU: Awesome.

00:24:32.010 --> 00:24:32.730
So--

00:24:32.730 --> 00:24:34.810
FELIPE HOFFA: Just to
give my little bit.

00:24:34.810 --> 00:24:35.310
Yes.

00:24:35.310 --> 00:24:38.080
Material learning is
completely the future

00:24:38.080 --> 00:24:40.380
and what everyone is
really focused right now.

00:24:40.380 --> 00:24:43.630
Everyone wants to learn tensor
flow, et cetera, et cetera.

00:24:43.630 --> 00:24:45.720
So that's pretty exciting
to see how things

00:24:45.720 --> 00:24:48.390
are going to get easier to use.

00:24:48.390 --> 00:24:52.320
But then at the same
time, I personally

00:24:52.320 --> 00:24:54.540
have not moved into the
material learning realm.

00:24:54.540 --> 00:24:58.590
I just think there is so
much more to do with data.

00:24:58.590 --> 00:25:02.160
Anything with material learning
will require data engineers.

00:25:02.160 --> 00:25:05.500
That's the name of the
Meetup, how do we move data?

00:25:05.500 --> 00:25:10.270
And I'm especially excited
about real time data.

00:25:10.270 --> 00:25:12.900
So we have [INAUDIBLE]
now available.

00:25:12.900 --> 00:25:16.710
I would really love to
see more public feeds

00:25:16.710 --> 00:25:21.840
of fresh livestreamed data and
people doing things with it,

00:25:21.840 --> 00:25:24.130
building pipelines over it.

00:25:24.130 --> 00:25:24.630
Totally.

00:25:24.630 --> 00:25:27.790
Material learning,
that's the hottest one.

00:25:27.790 --> 00:25:30.080
ANDREI SAVU: OK, so I will
ask, like, [INAUDIBLE]

00:25:30.080 --> 00:25:31.110
questions to go around.

00:25:31.110 --> 00:25:34.610
But then I want you to
think about your questions

00:25:34.610 --> 00:25:37.730
as we close down this session.

00:25:37.730 --> 00:25:44.960
So my final question for you
is, if you were able to go back

00:25:44.960 --> 00:25:50.550
based on what you know today
and tell a story to yourself,

00:25:50.550 --> 00:25:52.910
tell a bunch of advices,
what would that be?

00:25:56.094 --> 00:25:58.010
LUIS BITENCOURT: Think
a little bit better how

00:25:58.010 --> 00:26:00.170
to spread apart your datasets.

00:26:00.170 --> 00:26:03.680
We put everything, all of
our analytics of an event

00:26:03.680 --> 00:26:04.850
in one dataset.

00:26:04.850 --> 00:26:07.010
With BigQuery you
don't get the ability

00:26:07.010 --> 00:26:09.462
to do custom partitions
like you do in Hive,

00:26:09.462 --> 00:26:11.170
or hierarchical
partitions, so everything

00:26:11.170 --> 00:26:12.530
is partitioned by day.

00:26:12.530 --> 00:26:16.430
And it turns out that we just
generate too much data per day,

00:26:16.430 --> 00:26:18.890
even at BigQuery scales,
to be able to scan

00:26:18.890 --> 00:26:22.440
through one day's worth of
data in a reasonable time.

00:26:22.440 --> 00:26:26.900
So leveraging different
datasets as basically one level

00:26:26.900 --> 00:26:28.700
of partitioning
your data would have

00:26:28.700 --> 00:26:30.170
been a way to be much faster.

00:26:33.380 --> 00:26:36.550
We were way too much into
the denormalized camp

00:26:36.550 --> 00:26:40.210
and avoiding joins due to the
slowness of our Hive solutions

00:26:40.210 --> 00:26:41.520
there.

00:26:41.520 --> 00:26:44.270
And I have not had any issues
with joins in BigQuery,

00:26:44.270 --> 00:26:46.820
so I would be more open to
doing joins and separating out

00:26:46.820 --> 00:26:48.476
my data.

00:26:48.476 --> 00:26:51.750
ANDREI SAVU: That's a refreshing
thing to hear about big data

00:26:51.750 --> 00:26:52.760
and joins.

00:26:52.760 --> 00:26:56.630
Big data and joins
used to incompatible.

00:26:56.630 --> 00:26:59.150
ROBIN LI: I think for me,
if I want to redo it over,

00:26:59.150 --> 00:27:02.720
I probably want to have
data visualization,

00:27:02.720 --> 00:27:05.210
a lot more a richer
data visualizations

00:27:05.210 --> 00:27:09.170
to tell my story of how we're
dealing with data, how we're

00:27:09.170 --> 00:27:12.980
using data to drive
insights driving

00:27:12.980 --> 00:27:14.400
the revenue of the company.

00:27:14.400 --> 00:27:16.790
I think that's very important,
because lot of your data

00:27:16.790 --> 00:27:19.710
engineers do a lot of dirty
works and grunt works,

00:27:19.710 --> 00:27:24.930
but in order for your company
to be a data-driven company,

00:27:24.930 --> 00:27:27.320
you definitely need
a lot of insights

00:27:27.320 --> 00:27:29.540
and a lot of visualization
of your data.

00:27:29.540 --> 00:27:31.715
Not just a data sitting
on a spreadsheet.

00:27:34.390 --> 00:27:37.900
ANDREAS SEKINE: I think along
the same vein as visualization,

00:27:37.900 --> 00:27:43.780
we had a bug a little while
back where some of our front end

00:27:43.780 --> 00:27:46.330
servers stopped sending
event data to the back end

00:27:46.330 --> 00:27:48.670
and no one noticed for a while.

00:27:48.670 --> 00:27:50.200
For an embarrassingly long time.

00:27:50.200 --> 00:27:53.950
So realizing that not all
failures are catastrophic

00:27:53.950 --> 00:27:56.000
and that they could
be partial or subtle

00:27:56.000 --> 00:27:58.270
and having more rich
moderating and alerting

00:27:58.270 --> 00:28:00.520
to make sure that your data
is trustworthy and safe

00:28:00.520 --> 00:28:06.500
is something that is kind
of obvious in hindsight.

00:28:06.500 --> 00:28:07.340
ANDREI SAVU: Sweet.

00:28:07.340 --> 00:28:11.725
So any questions
from the audience?

00:28:11.725 --> 00:28:12.600
I think we all have--

00:28:17.204 --> 00:28:18.870
LUIS BITENCOURT: We
answered everything.

00:28:23.670 --> 00:28:25.130
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:28:25.130 --> 00:28:28.070
So my question was sort of
on the data governance side.

00:28:28.070 --> 00:28:31.910
And you mentioned in
terms of understanding

00:28:31.910 --> 00:28:35.600
which visualizations or business
questions you need to answer.

00:28:35.600 --> 00:28:40.760
What are some process-level
mechanisms or actual tools--

00:28:40.760 --> 00:28:44.420
like, do you have a
glossary of certain reports

00:28:44.420 --> 00:28:46.220
that you guys certify?

00:28:46.220 --> 00:28:50.495
Or what are some of the ways
you kind of manage, you know,

00:28:50.495 --> 00:28:52.100
we are getting
accurate data and it

00:28:52.100 --> 00:28:54.759
should be used for X, Y and Z?

00:28:54.759 --> 00:28:57.224
FELIPE HOFFA: You want to
repeat that question again?

00:28:57.224 --> 00:28:57.552
LUIS BITENCOURT: Oh.

00:28:57.552 --> 00:28:58.210
Yeah.

00:28:58.210 --> 00:29:01.170
Does that work?

00:29:01.170 --> 00:29:03.430
So the question was
on data governance,

00:29:03.430 --> 00:29:06.120
and I'm assuming, leading
into the QA side of it,

00:29:06.120 --> 00:29:07.110
things that break.

00:29:07.110 --> 00:29:09.070
But I'll add a
little bit more, too.

00:29:09.070 --> 00:29:12.015
There's all-around a
PII protection of data.

00:29:13.507 --> 00:29:15.090
While analytics is
great, you probably

00:29:15.090 --> 00:29:16.506
don't want everyone
in the company

00:29:16.506 --> 00:29:18.570
to have access to-- like,
on Reddit, for example,

00:29:18.570 --> 00:29:20.250
not everyone in the company
should know every single post

00:29:20.250 --> 00:29:22.230
that a particular
account can see, right?

00:29:22.230 --> 00:29:23.880
That's kind of creepy.

00:29:23.880 --> 00:29:27.560
There's IP information,
PII information,

00:29:27.560 --> 00:29:28.930
or a privacy policy.

00:29:28.930 --> 00:29:33.470
I think it's like 90 days where
we can keep PII on people.

00:29:33.470 --> 00:29:35.832
So things like an IP
address we keep for a while,

00:29:35.832 --> 00:29:37.290
but then we have
to go and mask it.

00:29:37.290 --> 00:29:40.920
So what happens when the scripts
to mask it or the items to mask

00:29:40.920 --> 00:29:43.090
it actually break and
you're no longer masking it?

00:29:43.090 --> 00:29:45.680
So a lot data governance
on Reddit, at least,

00:29:45.680 --> 00:29:48.180
is, first you've got to have a
breakdown of what data you're

00:29:48.180 --> 00:29:52.080
collecting, where you're
putting it and where it lives.

00:29:52.080 --> 00:29:53.820
One conversation we
were just having is,

00:29:53.820 --> 00:29:56.880
some people's [INAUDIBLE]
input data in different places,

00:29:56.880 --> 00:29:59.807
so there's also some ideas
of, OK, at some point,

00:29:59.807 --> 00:30:01.890
we need to start caring
about where the data lives

00:30:01.890 --> 00:30:03.515
outside of that to
make sure that we're

00:30:03.515 --> 00:30:08.110
protecting our users,
protecting their information.

00:30:08.110 --> 00:30:11.520
But the first step is an audit.

00:30:11.520 --> 00:30:13.387
Take an audit of where you are.

00:30:13.387 --> 00:30:15.720
Actually, the same way that
you get third party security

00:30:15.720 --> 00:30:17.386
audits, there's third
party data audits.

00:30:17.386 --> 00:30:21.007
So if you're at that
level of data governance,

00:30:21.007 --> 00:30:23.340
you can get a third party to
come in, look at your data,

00:30:23.340 --> 00:30:25.290
see what you're storing,
where you have it,

00:30:25.290 --> 00:30:27.230
where data leakage is.

00:30:27.230 --> 00:30:30.620
And it's actually not that
hard to accidentally leak data,

00:30:30.620 --> 00:30:32.250
at least within your company.

00:30:32.250 --> 00:30:33.350
There's so many processes.

00:30:33.350 --> 00:30:35.766
There's things that go to an
event collector, then a Kafka

00:30:35.766 --> 00:30:38.760
pipeline, and then we dump
it into S3 or GCS and then

00:30:38.760 --> 00:30:39.390
BigQuery.

00:30:39.390 --> 00:30:41.670
And any of those, if all
of a sudden other people

00:30:41.670 --> 00:30:43.450
have access to it
for other purposes,

00:30:43.450 --> 00:30:45.420
now they can pipe in and
look at data that they

00:30:45.420 --> 00:30:47.370
shouldn't be looking at.

00:30:47.370 --> 00:30:49.530
Then there's the
whole quality side.

00:30:49.530 --> 00:30:52.620
We've had the same bugs,
where Android Reddit

00:30:52.620 --> 00:30:54.204
app stopped sending events.

00:30:54.204 --> 00:30:55.620
I don't think it
was screen views,

00:30:55.620 --> 00:30:57.744
but one of our core events,
it just stopped sending

00:30:57.744 --> 00:31:00.300
and we didn't notice until a
couple of weeks after the build

00:31:00.300 --> 00:31:03.605
was out because the adoption
curve was a little bit smaller

00:31:03.605 --> 00:31:04.980
but we didn't have
a simple thing

00:31:04.980 --> 00:31:07.350
like a dashboard of
build-to-build or

00:31:07.350 --> 00:31:09.690
deploy-to-deploy, what
are your key events

00:31:09.690 --> 00:31:11.930
and which ones have
significant changes over those?

00:31:11.930 --> 00:31:13.470
So things like
that is another way

00:31:13.470 --> 00:31:16.560
to have governance,
but first step, audit.

00:31:16.560 --> 00:31:18.810
And the second one is, if
you don't have it automated,

00:31:18.810 --> 00:31:20.440
if you don't have
monitoring over it.

00:31:20.440 --> 00:31:22.530
So you have to have a set of,
what are my golden events?

00:31:22.530 --> 00:31:23.970
What are the ones
that can't break?

00:31:23.970 --> 00:31:25.830
And there should be a test
for every single one of those.

00:31:25.830 --> 00:31:27.840
If there's ever a deploy
and your delta of those

00:31:27.840 --> 00:31:30.904
goes above a certain amount,
you've got to fire an alert.

00:31:30.904 --> 00:31:33.570
If it's not automated, if you're
relying on people to notice it,

00:31:33.570 --> 00:31:34.500
you're not going to
catch it and you're

00:31:34.500 --> 00:31:35.541
going to miss those bugs.

00:31:40.830 --> 00:31:42.840
ANDREI SAVU: Any
other questions?

00:31:42.840 --> 00:31:45.910
I have another one around
the organization more

00:31:45.910 --> 00:31:46.870
than anything else.

00:31:46.870 --> 00:31:48.850
So it's kind of
related to governance.

00:31:48.850 --> 00:31:51.100
There are various ways in
which you can organize data.

00:31:51.100 --> 00:31:53.760
Google Cloud between
projects and folders

00:31:53.760 --> 00:31:58.020
and I'm wondering how do
you organize your data?

00:31:58.020 --> 00:32:01.200
Do you split storage
versus query, or not?

00:32:01.200 --> 00:32:03.960
Or what are some of
the things you do?

00:32:03.960 --> 00:32:05.070
LUIS BITENCOURT: Badly.

00:32:05.070 --> 00:32:10.560
To go back to what I would tell
myself, so we did two things.

00:32:10.560 --> 00:32:11.620
We versioned our events.

00:32:11.620 --> 00:32:13.328
So first thing when
we started at Reddit,

00:32:13.328 --> 00:32:15.509
there was schema-less
events, which is horrible.

00:32:15.509 --> 00:32:16.550
You always want a schema.

00:32:16.550 --> 00:32:17.400
You always want to enforce it.

00:32:17.400 --> 00:32:19.566
You want to be able to know
what fields are expected

00:32:19.566 --> 00:32:20.706
and what's not expected.

00:32:20.706 --> 00:32:22.080
So we had the
[INAUDIBLE] events.

00:32:22.080 --> 00:32:24.246
And for those we actually
broke them out by dataset,

00:32:24.246 --> 00:32:26.910
and we had one project for all
the events, different projects.

00:32:30.115 --> 00:32:32.647
So datasets are grouped by,
is it projects in BigQuery?

00:32:32.647 --> 00:32:34.230
FELIPE HOFFA: Project,
dataset, table.

00:32:34.230 --> 00:32:35.063
LUIS BITENCOURT: OK.

00:32:35.063 --> 00:32:39.412
So projects, I guess, is
the thing I was thinking of.

00:32:39.412 --> 00:32:40.620
Maybe I'm thinking of tables.

00:32:40.620 --> 00:32:41.130
Now I'm confused.

00:32:41.130 --> 00:32:42.810
Project is a Google
Cloud project, right?

00:32:42.810 --> 00:32:43.020
FELIPE HOFFA: Yeah.

00:32:43.020 --> 00:32:43.530
LUIS BITENCOURT: OK, sorry.

00:32:43.530 --> 00:32:45.000
So ignore what I was saying.

00:32:47.880 --> 00:32:51.140
Multiple tables is what I wanted
to have, not multiple datasets.

00:32:51.140 --> 00:32:53.756
I have one data set and one
table for all my V2 events,

00:32:53.756 --> 00:32:55.380
and then I can't
actually segment them.

00:32:55.380 --> 00:32:58.470
I need more granular than
a day's worth of data.

00:32:58.470 --> 00:33:02.820
So we have one project for
all of our production events.

00:33:02.820 --> 00:33:07.110
We have one project for
employee datasets, so things

00:33:07.110 --> 00:33:08.949
that are like ad hoc analytics.

00:33:08.949 --> 00:33:11.240
And those are literally
organized by the employee name,

00:33:11.240 --> 00:33:13.500
so we know exactly who to go
to and see if they're just

00:33:13.500 --> 00:33:16.000
trying out different things or
trying out different datasets

00:33:16.000 --> 00:33:18.990
and they're not polluting
the official project.

00:33:18.990 --> 00:33:21.590
And then we have now the idea
of these reporting tables

00:33:21.590 --> 00:33:23.520
and aggregate tables
which we're eventually

00:33:23.520 --> 00:33:27.690
forming into our golden dataset,
so what are the blessed tables

00:33:27.690 --> 00:33:30.570
and reports that we
have cleaned up data on,

00:33:30.570 --> 00:33:32.130
that we've got them
pre-aggregated so

00:33:32.130 --> 00:33:34.860
that we can do queries really
fast on the business side.

00:33:40.015 --> 00:33:41.890
ANDREAS SEKINE: So we
definitely started down

00:33:41.890 --> 00:33:44.320
this path on Google
Cloud Storage where

00:33:44.320 --> 00:33:46.841
we were trying to not
have too many buckets,

00:33:46.841 --> 00:33:49.090
and we realized that was the
wrong decision, that it's

00:33:49.090 --> 00:33:50.950
better to have more
buckets because they

00:33:50.950 --> 00:33:53.680
are a pretty singular
unit of control

00:33:53.680 --> 00:33:55.870
over, whether it's
lifecycle management

00:33:55.870 --> 00:34:00.220
or permissions or
the storage class,

00:34:00.220 --> 00:34:04.209
buckets tend to be the way
that GCS has you control them.

00:34:04.209 --> 00:34:06.250
So we've started moving
more towards more buckets

00:34:06.250 --> 00:34:09.070
with fewer folders within them.

00:34:09.070 --> 00:34:12.880
As far as BigQuery goes, we
do have particular blessed

00:34:12.880 --> 00:34:15.610
datasets where they have what we
consider analytics data, which

00:34:15.610 --> 00:34:18.040
is kind of the nicely
sanitized, cleaned,

00:34:18.040 --> 00:34:23.110
pre-joined data which anyone
who has kind of more cursory

00:34:23.110 --> 00:34:25.780
or casual questions about
key metrics of the company

00:34:25.780 --> 00:34:29.080
can query those tables and trust
that the analytics teams have

00:34:29.080 --> 00:34:34.120
done their job to make sure it's
sane data and clean in there.

00:34:34.120 --> 00:34:35.770
ROBIN LI: I guess
another benefits

00:34:35.770 --> 00:34:39.190
when you have multiple
projects or buckets, which

00:34:39.190 --> 00:34:44.199
is cost control array, so you
can structure your projects

00:34:44.199 --> 00:34:46.239
into different tiers
and different tiers

00:34:46.239 --> 00:34:51.190
can have different
capability to run queries.

00:34:51.190 --> 00:34:54.800
So you can set up caps on
different buckets as well.

00:34:54.800 --> 00:34:57.690
So that's another way of doing
good cost control in BigQuery.

00:35:00.790 --> 00:35:04.150
FELIPE HOFFA: I have two
questions, action-item-related.

00:35:04.150 --> 00:35:08.050
One is what should I
tell my product managers?

00:35:08.050 --> 00:35:11.556
What change would you
like from Google Cloud?

00:35:11.556 --> 00:35:13.350
Excellent.

00:35:13.350 --> 00:35:16.900
And the other question,
if someone here

00:35:16.900 --> 00:35:21.526
wants a weekend project, what
would you recommend them to do?

00:35:21.526 --> 00:35:23.400
What would be a fun
project for the audience?

00:35:25.970 --> 00:35:27.550
LUIS BITENCOURT:
Custom partitions.

00:35:27.550 --> 00:35:29.750
hierarchical
partitions in BigQuery.

00:35:29.750 --> 00:35:32.950
That would save my life.

00:35:32.950 --> 00:35:37.000
And weekend
projects, I would say

00:35:37.000 --> 00:35:39.310
if you're looking for extra
projects, Reddit is hiring.

00:35:39.310 --> 00:35:40.893
So you should just
talk to me and I'll

00:35:40.893 --> 00:35:43.720
pay you to do your
weekend project.

00:35:43.720 --> 00:35:47.140
If you don't want to
get paid for the work,

00:35:47.140 --> 00:35:48.070
I literally think--

00:35:48.070 --> 00:35:49.986
I mean, I know it's more
the data engineering.

00:35:49.986 --> 00:35:53.270
But I think ML is huge.

00:35:53.270 --> 00:35:54.774
It's going to be even bigger.

00:35:54.774 --> 00:35:56.940
And if you want to be a
data engineer in the future,

00:35:56.940 --> 00:35:59.930
likelihood is going to be a
data engineer for ML projects.

00:35:59.930 --> 00:36:03.040
So I would pick whatever
thing you are manually

00:36:03.040 --> 00:36:06.370
doing today, some repetitive
process, some classification

00:36:06.370 --> 00:36:08.260
thing, and go play
and figure out

00:36:08.260 --> 00:36:10.540
if you can actually create
some stupid, dumb models

00:36:10.540 --> 00:36:12.778
to be able to predict
something for you there.

00:36:18.790 --> 00:36:22.180
ROBIN LI: There is one really,
really nice feature about

00:36:22.180 --> 00:36:24.950
BigQuery I really like,
which is your HyperLog++.

00:36:27.470 --> 00:36:30.830
I'm not sure if you guys all
know what HyperLogLog does.

00:36:30.830 --> 00:36:39.110
It's basically an algorithm
that developed in 1989.

00:36:39.110 --> 00:36:42.740
It's sort of seven years
younger than my age.

00:36:42.740 --> 00:36:45.950
But basically what
it does is trying

00:36:45.950 --> 00:36:50.370
to do an estimation
of our distinct values

00:36:50.370 --> 00:36:52.280
over your datasets.

00:36:52.280 --> 00:36:54.080
And Google takes it
up to another level,

00:36:54.080 --> 00:36:56.300
which is that you publish
a white paper about

00:36:56.300 --> 00:37:03.410
HyperLogLog++, which, typically
if you see our original

00:37:03.410 --> 00:37:07.100
algorithm, it gave you
the accuracy about 0.5%,

00:37:07.100 --> 00:37:09.920
which means if you actually
[INAUDIBLE] distinct values,

00:37:09.920 --> 00:37:13.310
and also using the HyperLogLog
algorithm to count the distinct

00:37:13.310 --> 00:37:17.660
values, the difference
should be around 0.5%.

00:37:17.660 --> 00:37:21.320
But in Google's case,
it's about 0.05%.

00:37:21.320 --> 00:37:23.990
It's really impressive
how accurate it is.

00:37:23.990 --> 00:37:26.300
And also Google has
a really nice feature

00:37:26.300 --> 00:37:32.970
which you can starting to
partition your distinct values

00:37:32.970 --> 00:37:37.730
into synopsis or sketches,
which means, let's say, if I--

00:37:37.730 --> 00:37:39.800
previously, if you want
to count last seven

00:37:39.800 --> 00:37:41.690
days of distinct
users, what you need

00:37:41.690 --> 00:37:43.670
to do is you always
need to get all

00:37:43.670 --> 00:37:45.170
the users for the
last seven days

00:37:45.170 --> 00:37:47.630
and starting to count these
things of them, right?

00:37:47.630 --> 00:37:51.590
But now in Google BigQuery what
you can do is, for each day

00:37:51.590 --> 00:37:54.130
you can count the
distinct values

00:37:54.130 --> 00:37:59.120
and put them into a hash string
and a story for that day.

00:37:59.120 --> 00:38:01.040
Do it for the next
six days and when

00:38:01.040 --> 00:38:03.980
you want to look back for the
last seven days of distinct,

00:38:03.980 --> 00:38:06.680
you just need to merge
all of the last seven days

00:38:06.680 --> 00:38:10.040
together, which saves you
a lot of conditional power.

00:38:10.040 --> 00:38:12.380
So that's one of the
nicest features I really

00:38:12.380 --> 00:38:15.980
like in Google BigQuery.

00:38:15.980 --> 00:38:18.970
Other MPP has the same
feature, but Google

00:38:18.970 --> 00:38:23.060
in terms of speed and accuracy
is the best I've ever seen.

00:38:26.250 --> 00:38:28.500
ANDREAS SEKINE: Not so much
